// The backlog's own rule set, and it is deliberately narrow.
//
// `docs/` is a vault. The backlog owner opens it in Obsidian, writes notes, and commits
// them to `main` — and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on a push to `main`, while
// `release.yml` will not publish until that run's `verify` check is green. So a Markdown
// rule that fires on ordinary typing does not "keep the register tidy": it turns main red
// for somebody who was writing a note, and blocks the next release until they learn a rule
// nobody told them about. The root `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc` keeps its full set for the
// files a CONTRIBUTOR maintains — the root documents, `.github/`, and this repository's own
// three skills. This file is what the OWNER writes under.
//
// What survives is the class where the note means something other than what it says, or
// loses content outright. Everything else is a convention here, not a gate. The one-time
// normalisation that ADR 0032 records is kept; what is given up is enforcing it on notes
// written from now on, which is the price of the owner's own workflow and is cheaper than
// the workflow.
//
// **Which rules those are was measured against Obsidian's own documentation, not guessed.**
// `test/docs/markdownScope.test.ts` plants a note using every construct the syntax pages
// document — wikilinks, aliases, embeds, block references, comments, highlights, callouts
// in all three forms, footnotes, task lists, Tab-nested lists, math and Mermaid — and
// asserts this file reports nothing on it. Under `default: true` that same note draws
// SEVEN findings from four rules, every one of them on a shape Obsidian itself prints as
// the correct way to write:
//
//   - MD032 (blanks-around-lists) on a list inside a callout, which is the multi-paragraph
//     callout example on <https://obsidian.md/help/callouts> verbatim.
//   - MD028 (no-blanks-blockquote) on the blank line between two adjacent callouts.
//   - MD007 (ul-indent) and MD010 (no-hard-tabs) on Tab-indented nesting, which
//     <https://obsidian.md/help/syntax> names as THE way to nest a list: "indent list items
//     with `Tab` or `Shift+Tab`".
//
// That is why those four are off, and it is a stronger reason than the one this file used
// to give. Obsidian's own **style guide** (<https://obsidian.md/help/style-guide>) does ask
// for the opposite of the first — "use newlines between Markdown blocks" — and it is not
// adopted here for two reasons: it is the house style of Obsidian's DOCUMENTATION team for
// help.obsidian.md, not a rule about vaults, and MD032, the rule that would enforce it,
// fails on Obsidian's own callout example. A gate cannot hold a vault to a style whose
// checker rejects the vault's own documented syntax.
//
// **One exception to the blank-line reasoning, and no rule anywhere can gate it.** An
// ORDERED list may interrupt a paragraph only when it starts at `1.`, so a `Steps:` line
// directly above `2. …` swallows the whole list into the paragraph. That is content loss,
// not typing style — but it is invisible to markdownlint for the reason it is content loss:
// the linter parses the document the renderer parses, both see one paragraph, and MD032 has
// no list to be unsurrounded. Checked with `default: true`, which reports nothing on it. So
// it is a gap in the gate rather than a rule left off, and ADR 0032 carries it as a revisit
// condition rather than this comment promising more than the rules below deliver.
//
// **Nothing here relaxes `docs-check.mjs`.** The register's own rules — hierarchy, sibling
// orders, wikilinks that resolve, source paths that exist, the use-case shape, ADR
// frontmatter — are gated exactly as before, and they are the rules that are actually about
// this backlog. This file governs Markdown syntax alone.
{
	"default": false,

	// A row with more cells than the header silently DROPS the extra ones, in Obsidian and
	// on GitHub alike — GFM splits a row on `|` before it parses anything inline, so a
	// code span does not protect a pipe inside it. This is the rule that earned the gate:
	// `docs/requirements/View options and config warnings.md` had been showing a
	// three-column row with two cells missing since it was written.
	//
	// It is also the one rule here that guards a mistake Obsidian's own documentation warns
	// about. <https://obsidian.md/help/advanced-syntax> says a wikilink alias or an image
	// size inside a table needs the pipe escaped — `[[A note\|shown text]]`,
	// `![[Engelbart.jpg\|200]]` — and an owner who forgets loses the cell. Checked against
	// markdownlint 0.41.1 both ways: the escaped form passes, and the unescaped
	// `[[A note|shown text]]` in a table reports `table-column-count`.
	"MD056": true,
	// MD055 (table-pipe-style) was here until 2026-08-30 and is deliberately OUT. It was
	// added as "the same loss from the other direction", which GFM does not do: outer pipes
	// are optional and are stripped before the cells are counted, so a row that omits one
	// parses with the same cell count as its neighbours. What MD055 actually fires on is a
	// table whose rows do not AGREE about outer pipes — a style question, and one that
	// would turn main red for an owner whose note renders correctly. Checked both ways
	// against markdownlint 0.41.1: a consistently pipe-less table passes MD055; a row
	// genuinely short or long a cell is caught by MD056 above, with or without outer pipes.

	// `(text)[url]` is a link written backwards. It renders as literal text, so the
	// destination is on screen and unclickable, and nothing else reports it:
	// `docs-check.mjs` resolves the links it can SEE, and this is not one.
	"MD011": true,
	// A link with no destination at all — `[like this]()` — which reads as a link and goes
	// nowhere.
	"MD042": true,
	// A footnote or reference definition nothing points at. The text is written and never
	// renders, which is the same loss as the two rules above rather than a style question,
	// and it cannot fire on typing: it takes a `[^x]:` definition and then no `[^x]` to
	// reach it. Obsidian documents footnotes as first-class syntax, so the owner may well
	// write one.
	//
	// Admitted on the principle rather than on an instance, and that is stated rather than
	// implied: the vault contains no footnote today, so this rule has never fired here. So
	// have MD011 and MD042 — neither has ever had an instance either, and both are in for
	// the same reason. Checked for the false positive that would matter: a footnote
	// referenced from inside a callout or a table is seen, and footnote-shaped text inside a
	// code span, a fenced block, an inline `^[footnote]` or an Obsidian block id `^abc123`
	// is not mistaken for one.
	"MD053": true
}
